=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The reported fault does not exist. A thorough inspection of the entire OSPF core — r1 (Router ID 10.0.0.76), csw1 (10.0.0.77), csw2 (10.0.0.78), dswa1 (10.0.0.79), dswa2 (10.0.0.80), dswb1 (10.0.0.81), and dswb2 (10.0.0.82) — shows every adjacency in Full state with no evidence of bouncing.

The r1–csw1 adjacency (eth0/10.0.0.33 ↔ 10.0.0.34) and the r1–csw2 adjacency (eth1/10.0.0.37 ↔ 10.0.0.38) were sampled three times over approximately four minutes. Up-times incremented consistently from ~42 s → ~1 m 42 s → ~4 m 01 s, which is the expected linear progression of a stable relationship — not the reset-to-near-zero pattern that would indicate a flap. The FRR log on csw1 contains exactly two initialization lines (20:36:20) and one Database Description negotiation event (20:37:08), which is the single normal exchange that occurs when an adjacency first forms. There are no subsequent log entries — zero adjacency drops, zero state-machine transitions.

The short initial up-times (~42–52 s) that may have triggered the alert are fully explained by the Kathara lab having just started: FRR initialized at 20:36:20 and the first adjacencies completed around 20:37:05, exactly matching those values. OSPF hello/dead timers are uniformly Hello 10 s / Dead 40 s on every interface; there are no authentication or area mismatches; no cron jobs, kill scripts, or watchfrr-kill injections are present on any device.

Root cause: there is no fault. The "vague alert" was triggered by the normal burst of SPF activity (13–21 runs) during initial multi-router convergence of a freshly started lab, not by ongoing instability.

Corrective action: none required. Notify network operations that the alert was a false positive from lab startup; no adjacency needs to be restarted.
=== END ===